      "id": 1,
      "text": "Montague Center, the town’s original European settlement, was once part of Sunderland before becoming the nucleus of the 18th‑century community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montague%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Turners Falls, one of Montague’s five villages, evolved from a canal‑powered mill town into a planned industrial community in the mid‑1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montague%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s landscape spans 31.5 square miles of rolling hills, fertile farmlands, historic mills, and residential villages.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://montague-ma.gov/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "vague"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Montague’s name honors its first European settlers, who arrived in 1715 and officially incorporated the town in 1754.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montague%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Connecticut River borders Montague to the west, while the Millers River drains the town’s interior.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montague%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
